# Test: a config reload does NOT tear down an established DYNAMIC peer.
#
# The peer takes the ACTIVE role and dials ze (--dial), because a dynamic peer is
# the one topology a listening test peer cannot express: the group is `ip dynamic`
# + `connect false`, so ze only ever ACCEPTS.
#
# The configuration names NO peer at all. That is the route-server shape, and it
# is what makes the dynamic path load-bearing here: the only socket in the run is
# the one the GROUP asks for (startMultiListeners, reactor.go), and the only peer
# is the one the inbound connection creates (tryCreateDynamicPeer,
# reactor_dynamic.go).
#
# VALIDATES: a reload that does not touch the dynamic group leaves its established
#            sessions alone.
# PREVENTS: the teardown of EVERY dynamic session on EVERY reload.
#           reconcilePeersJournaled (internal/component/bgp/reactor/reactor_api.go)
#           diffed the config-derived peer list against every entry of r.peers. A
#           dynamic peer's key is the AddrPort it CONNECTED FROM and no config
#           names it, so it read as "no longer configured" and was stopped. An IXP
#           or route-server operator lost every session on any reload, including a
#           reload that changed nothing about them.
#
# DISCRIMINATION: the assertion after the reload is POSITIVE and it is about
# CONTINUITY, not about state. "The session is established afterwards" would pass
# against the defect, because the peer would simply re-connect. Here the peer
# dials ONCE and never re-dials, so every byte asserted below arrives on the ONE
# TCP connection the OPEN was exchanged on.
#
# The continuity carrier is the shutdown NOTIFICATION. SIGTERM makes ze send Cease
# / Administrative Shutdown to every ESTABLISHED session before dropping it
# (RFC 4271 Section 8.2.2 ManualStop, asserted on its own in signal-stop-cease.ci),
# and it reaches the peers ze still HOLDS. Under the defect the reload has already
# stopped this peer and closed its connection, so the peer reads EOF and fails with
# "connection closed before completion" instead of these octets.
#
# The signals come from trigger.sh rather than from peer actions: ze-peer waits a
# fixed 500ms after signalling (pauseForSignal, internal/test/peer/peer.go) and a
# reload of this config takes longer than that, so a peer-driven SIGTERM could
# reach the daemon while the reload it is meant to follow was still running.

# "dynamic peer created" is an Info line and the daemon's base level is warn
# (ze.log, internal/core/slogutil/slogutil.go), so the assertion at the foot of
# this file sees nothing without this.
option=env:var=ze.log.bgp.reactor:value=info

# ze-peer expectations
stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=asn:value=65010
option=tcp_connections:value=1

# One message on the wire, after the reload, on the SAME connection: Cease
# (code 6) / Administrative Shutdown (subcode 2, RFC 4486) with the RFC 8203
# shutdown communication -- one length octet (0x17) and the 23-character default
# text "Administrative Shutdown" (Session.Teardown).
#
# End-of-RIB is not asserted here, and the reason is now scope rather than
# racing. Until 2026-08-13 a dynamic peer negotiated no address family at all --
# buildDynamicGroupSettings (internal/component/bgp/config/peers.go) never read
# the group's `family` block -- so the only End-of-RIB producer was a plugin's
# AnnounceEOR, dropped whenever it landed while the peer was still draining its
# initial sync (logEORSuppressed, reactor_api_forward.go): measured over 8 runs
# of this file, the marker arrived in 7 and was dropped in 1. The families are
# built now, so sendInitialRoutes emits the marker for each negotiated family and
# it no longer races. That negotiation is the subject of
# test/plugin/dynamic-peer-negotiates-configured-families.ci, which asserts the
# OPEN and both markers on the wire. This file is about a RELOAD, so it keeps the
# daemon's own establishment line below as its proxy.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF002D0306021741646D696E6973747261746976652053687574646F776E
EOF_PEER

# Initial ze configuration: one dynamic group and nothing else. tmpfs, not stdin:
# the daemon must read a FILE that trigger.sh can rewrite under it, and the runner
# writes daemon.pid into the same directory.
tmpfs=ze-bgp.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
bgp {
	group ix {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip dynamic
				connect false
				range 127.0.0.0/8
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept true
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65533
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast {
					mode enable
					prefix {
						maximum 10000
					}
				}
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

# Rewritten config: one static peer that never connects. The dynamic group is
# byte-identical, so nothing about the running dynamic session changed. The added
# peer is what makes the reload a real reconcile rather than a no-op.
tmpfs=config2.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF2
bgp {
	peer spare {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.3
				connect false
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept true
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65533
				remote 65533
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
	}

	group ix {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip dynamic
				connect false
				range 127.0.0.0/8
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept true
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65533
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast {
					mode enable
					prefix {
						maximum 10000
					}
				}
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF2

# Trigger: wait for the daemon, let the dynamic peer establish, reload, then stop.
# The gap between the reload and the shutdown is what makes the NOTIFICATION
# evidence of survival rather than of timing: the reload has finished by then, so
# a peer the reload would have torn down is already gone.
#
# Neither sleep carries the verdict, and neither one can make this file pass for
# the wrong reason. A first sleep too short for the session to establish trips the
# reject below, because the peer is then built after the signal. A second one too
# short for the reconcile to finish leaves "sighup reload complete" unwritten and
# trips the expect. The second is the longer one because this suite shares a
# machine with other test runs and a reload of this config was measured taking
# over 3s under that load, while the first only has to cover a dial the peer
# retries every 500ms against a daemon that has already written daemon.ready.
tmpfs=trigger.sh:mode=755:terminator=EOF_TRIGGER
#!/bin/sh
while [ ! -f daemon.pid ]; do sleep 0.1; done
while [ ! -f daemon.ready ]; do sleep 0.1; done
sleep 3
cp config2.conf ze-bgp.conf
kill -HUP $(cat daemon.pid)
sleep 8
kill -TERM $(cat daemon.pid)
EOF_TRIGGER

# Process orchestration. The peer is the background process, as in every other
# reload test, so the runner collects its output and drives the verdict from it.
# It binds no listener, so it retries the dial until ze binds (dialTarget,
# internal/test/peer/peer.go).
cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze-peer --dial 127.0.0.1:$PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=background:seq=2:exec=sh trigger.sh
cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=ze start ze-bgp.conf:timeout=30s

# The session above really was a DYNAMIC peer's. Without this the wire assertion
# alone would pass for any peer holding an open socket, which is how the first
# attempt at this test passed against unfixed code: the inbound was attributed to
# a statically configured peer by the listener it arrived on, and no dynamic peer
# was ever built.
expect=stderr:contains=dynamic peer created

# ... and it reached Established, which the NOTIFICATION above does not say on its
# own: ShutdownNotify (reactor/peer.go) is state-blind by design, so OpenSent and
# OpenConfirm receive that same message.
expect=stderr:contains=session established

# The reload really happened. Without this the whole file could pass on a run
# where the SIGHUP never arrived, which is the one way the assertions above are
# satisfied by a daemon that was never asked to reconcile anything.
expect=stderr:contains=sighup reload complete

# ... and the peer was built BEFORE it, once, and never again. The anchor is the
# arrival of the signal rather than the end of the reload, because both failures
# this rejects live on that side of it:
#
#   - a peer created DURING the reload proves the session under test post-dates
#     the reconcile, so nothing above says what the reconcile did to a session it
#     found already running. A daemon that opened no listening socket until the
#     reload built one passes every other line in this file that way.
#   - a peer created AFTER it is what a teardown looks like from the daemon's
#     side: the wire assertions cannot tell a surviving session from a re-dialed
#     one on their own.
reject=stderr:pattern=received SIGHUP[\s\S]*dynamic peer created

# The same anchor for establishment, so the session the reload met was already up
# rather than one that raced it.
reject=stderr:pattern=received SIGHUP[\s\S]*session established
